# Conventional Commits

```text
type(optional-scope): subject

optional body

optional footer
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`. A speed or size change is `feat` or `fix` with its measurement in the body; there is no `perf` type.
- Scope: English, a short noun for the area. Examples, not a closed list: `token`, `crypto`, `verify`, `machine-id`, `client`, `server`, `hosted`, `cli`, `rules`, `deps`, `ci`.
- Subject: English, imperative, no trailing period. The type and scope are always English (`.cursor/rules/language.mdc`).
- Breaking change: `type(scope)!: subject` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
- A closing keyword (`Closes #N`) never goes in a commit message, its subject or its body. A PR is merged with a merge commit (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`, step 7), so every commit of the branch lands on `main`, and GitHub closes an Issue that a closing keyword in any of them names: "You can also use closing keywords in a commit message. The issue will be closed when you merge the commit into the default branch" (GitHub Docs, Linking a pull request to an issue). Write `Refs #N` or `Part of #N` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`).
- Every commit takes one of the types above, the ones in the middle of a row too, and ends with the `Co-Authored-By` line when an agent wrote it. There is no `wip`. A commit that answers a review also stays on `main` with its own message. Some commits keep the subject that git, GitHub, or Dependabot gives them: the merge of `origin/main` into a branch (`.cursor/rules/git.mdc`), "Merge remote-tracking branch 'origin/main' into <branch>"; the merge that GitHub's "Update branch" button makes, "Merge branch 'main' into <branch>"; a PR's merge that the owner makes on GitHub, "Merge pull request #<n> from <owner>/<branch>"; and Dependabot's commits, such as b79cfb2 "Bump the test group with 2 updates" (PR #484), since `.github/dependabot.yml` sets no `commit-message` prefix. A merge an agent makes on the owner's order takes the message of `.cursor/rules/git.mdc`.
- A commit's type is the kind of change it makes, read from its own files, not the kind of the row: `feat` and `fix` change the product's code (`src/`, `web/admin/`, `include/`), and a commit that changes only documents, samples, and tests is `docs` or `test`. A PR merged with a merge commit keeps every commit of its branch on `main`. In V1d30 5845edb, which wrote `TimeoutStopSec=60` into the sample unit, the how-to pages, the design, the roadmap, the row's record, and an end-to-end test, took `feat(server)`; PR #532 was merged with the merge commit f122102, so it stays on `main` with that type.

```text
feat(verify): reject a token whose key ID is not in the trust store
fix(server): bind a lease renewal to the client key that checked it out
chore(rules): record the constant-time compare rule from the retrospective
```

```text
# BAD — the branch's second commit says "Closes #12" while the PR body says "Part of #12"; the merge commit brings that commit to main and closes #12
# GOOD — every commit of the branch says "Refs #12"; only the line under the PR body's ## Related decides whether #12 closes
```
